{"id": "DfE_JrizD0N", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "The Shaligram in your puja room is a 150-MILLION-year-old fossil 🐚 Did you know? Comment YES or NO 👇\n\nHindus worship the black Shaligram stone as Vishnu himself. The Devi Bhagavatam (9.24) tells how: Vishnu broke the heart of his devotee Tulsi, and she cursed him, “Your heart is stone. Become a stone!” Vishnu accepted. Her body became the river Gandaki, her hair the tulsi plant, and he stayed on her bank as a stone, where tiny creatures with sharp teeth carve his chakras.\n\nNow the science: crack a Shaligram open and there’s a spiral inside. It’s an ammonite, a sea creature of the Tethys sea that lived about 150 million years ago, in the age of the dinosaurs. When India crashed into Asia, that sea floor rose and became the Himalayas, and today these fossils wash out of one river: the Kali Gandaki in Nepal.\n\nSame river. Same stone. Same spiral.\n\nSo in a Shaligram puja you worship a piece of the Himalayas, with a tulsi leaf and river water. Krishna says in the Gita (10.25): “Of all that stands still, I am the Himalaya.” In Sanatan Dharma, nature itself is sacred. That’s why a Shaligram is never worshipped without tulsi. And this Kartik, on Tulsi Vivah, the tulsi plant and the Shaligram are married 🌿\n\nWant your kids to grow up knowing stories like this? Our picture books bring the gods to life for ages 3–10. Books 1–3, set of 3 for ₹500 at rishikatha.com (link in bio).\n\n💾 Save this for your next puja\n📤 Share it with someone who has a Shaligram at home\n💬 Did you know it was a fossil? YES or NO?\n\n#shaligram #saligram #vishnu #tulsi #tulsivivah gandaki muktinath himalayas ammonite fossil sanatandharma hinduism bhagavadgita hindumythology rishikatha", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1793276263, "like_count": 1812, "comment_count": 14, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "da